 systems make decisions, and how those decisions shape economic outcomes. My work combines economics, behavioral methods, and artificial intelligence to examine what happens when decisions are made under uncertainty, when people respond to machines, and when multiple AI agents interact with one another.\n\nMy current research includes simulations of Federal Reserve policy meetings, experiments on how AI agents behave in financial markets, and new ways to measure how people use and value AI. Across these projects, I use language models not only as tools, but also as objects of economic study.\n\nBefore joining Stanford, I spent seven years as a financial economist and quant at the Federal Reserve Bank of Richmond, where I worked on bank supervision and financial risk. That experience continues to shape how I think about institutions, incentives, and decision making.\n\n### Research Interests\n\nAI Agents, AI & Economic Measurement, Behavioral Finance, Policy Communication\n\nDownload CV\n\n### Recent & Upcoming\n\n  * Jul 17·Presented FOMC In Silico at the NBER Summer Institute\n  * Jul 10·\"Financial Stability in the Age of AI\" at the EDSS conference, Banque de France\n  * Jun 27·Keynote at the South African Reserve Bank: \"The New Financial Stability Problem\"\n  * Jun 2·Bank of Korea: talk on the use of AI by central banks\n  * May 19·Invited seminar at the National Bank of Belgium\n  * May 8·Presenting FOMC In Silico at the 2nd Workshop on AI Behavioral Science, Stanford SIEPR\n  * Apr 24·NYU Stern 7th Annual Volatility and Risk Institute Conference: \"AI and Risk Management\"\n  * Apr 13–14·Presented SurplusGPT at AI and the Economy Inaugural Workshop, Penn State--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n13th ECB Conference on Forecasting Techniques (https://acesso.kissmyscore.com/?_=%2Fpress%2Fconferences%2Fhtml%2F20260323_13th_conference_on_forecasting_techniques.sv.html%23xybe3UUSZzbs022gGyK6e8on2MJGSWZ87g%3D%3D)\ncite